            "text": "At dawn, the city forgot its name. Rain moved through the streets like a patient hand, polishing windows, darkening brick, waking the green things that lived between cracks. Somewhere, a baker lifted a metal shutter, and warm bread entered the weather. People emerged carrying umbrellas that bloomed like flowers. A child splashed deliberately in a puddle, laughing whenever a bus sent water over the curb. No one complained. For once, everyone seemed to understand that the morning belonged to nobody and therefore to everyone.\n\nOn the corner, an man fed crumbs to pigeons. One bird, missing a feather from its left wing, watched him with solemn attention. He spoke to it as though they shared an old secret. Perhaps they did. Perhaps every creature collects small proofs that it has survived: a warm room, a voice, the taste of something sweet. Perhaps survival is less about strength than remembering where comfort waits.\n\nBy noon, the rain had stopped. Sunlight spilled across the pavement, revealing coins, leaves, and a blue button someone had lost. A woman picked it up and held it against her coat. For a moment, she looked dressed for a different life. Then she put the button in her pocket and continued walking.\n\nThat evening, the city would resume its usual noise: horns, arguments, elevators, music leaking through walls. Yet beneath everything, rainwater would continue its quiet journey, slipping toward the river. It would carry dust, petals, and reflections, making no distinction between what was broken and what was beautiful."
          }
        ],
